If you’re honest, a few things in your life probably feel loose right now. Nothing has completely fallen apart. Yet. **But a few things feel loose:**

- A friendship you counted on has slowly gone quiet, and you can’t point to the moment it changed.
- Work feels shaky, and you’ve wondered if your job is safe.
- A family relationship is strained over politics, race, religion, or even a sports team.

For me, it’s an apology I never gave a friend.&nbsp;

When it happens, you notice how much of your security was resting on those things: your friendships, your job, your family, your team identity, and underneath all of that, **your sense of who you are.**&nbsp;

Then the question you don’t want to say out loud shows up: what, if anything, still holds?

It reminds me of Elizabeth Zott in the TV series *Lessons in Chemistry*. She keeps showing up, but the lab won’t make room for her because she’s a woman. The systems that were supposed to support the science keep shutting her out. She has to keep deciding what still holds when the structures won’t. (Spoiler: she figures it out.)&nbsp;

Amos 9 starts in a similar place, only worse. God is standing beside the altar. The doorframes are shaking, and the whole structure is coming down on the people. Nobody gets out.&nbsp;

Then we hit the last few verses, and the whole tone changes.

Finally, after all these weeks studying the Book of Amos, we get some real good and lasting news.

The same God who spends most of this chapter, and nearly all of the eight before it, on judgment spends the last part raising up what has fallen and planting his people so they will never be uprooted again. The judgment is real, but it doesn’t get the last word.

But first, the hard part.

### **The Judgment That Reaches**

“Not one of them shall flee away; not one of them shall escape” (Amos 9:1).

Amos lists every hiding place. Dig down into the ground. Climb up to heaven. Hide on a mountain or at the bottom of the sea. Go into captivity. None of it works.

This is like Psalm 139 turned inside out. David asks, “Where shall I go from your Spirit? Or where shall I flee from your presence?” and finds comfort in the answer: nowhere. The answer is the same, but now it’s scary.

Some people had been telling themselves, “Disaster shall not overtake or meet us” (v. 10 ESV). God had already answered that. He brought Israel out of Egypt, but he also moved other nations from place to place.&nbsp;

**Being chosen didn’t mean being exempt.**

But the judgment isn’t total. God says he will shake Israel like grain in a sieve, yet no pebble will fall to the ground (vv. 8-9). Verse 1 says not one escapes. Verse 9 says not one is lost.

Like many of the chapters before it, this one tempts me to dodge the part aimed at me. I’d rather stay with the carefully managed version of my life that shows up in front of people and at work (and yes, even at church), and keep the rest out of public view.&nbsp;

But if God is everywhere and nothing is hidden, then what I keep out of public view isn’t hidden from him either.&nbsp;

### **The Booth That Rises**

Then comes the turn: “In that day I will raise up the booth of David that is fallen… and rebuild it as in the days of old” (v. 11).

A booth is just a temporary shelter. Fragile, easy to knock down. That’s what Amos calls David’s royal line, which once ruled a whole kingdom and was now barely hanging on. But God says he’ll rebuild it. And centuries later, the heir to that line shows up as a baby in a feeding trough and ends on a cross that looks like a complete failure.&nbsp;

This rebuilt booth has room for more than Israel. It includes people from Edom, the enemy Amos condemned earlier, and all the nations called by God’s name (v. 12). James quotes this promise in Acts 15 when the early church welcomes Gentiles.

The chapter ends with ordinary life. Ruined cities get rebuilt. People plant vineyards and actually drink the wine they grow. That flips an earlier warning in the book, where people planted but never got to enjoy the harvest. Now God plants his people, and “they shall never again be uprooted” (v. 15).

In Demi Lovato’s “Skyscraper,” a city lies flattened and one tower is still standing in the rubble. The song is a dare: go ahead, try to knock me down. That tower is holding itself up. But in Amos, the booth is already flat on the ground, and God is the one ***who picks it up***.

***Some days your life feels more like the booth on the ground than the skyscraper.***&nbsp;

The promise isn’t that everything will look strong right now. It’s that what God raises and plants will hold.

### **The People Who Remain**

The God who touches the earth and it melts (v. 5) is the same God who rebuilds the booth. His power means his promises hold.

The booth of David has been raised in Jesus, the greater Son of David. So what happens to the judgment Amos describes? It falls on him. He was crucified in weakness and raised in power, and in him the last promise is secure: his people will never be uprooted.

Finally, some good news. The Judge is also the Restorer. The day that starts in judgment ends with a people planted securely by the God who keeps every promise.

### **So What Does It Mean for You (and me!)?**

That means you don’t get to assume disaster will skip you just because you go to church or call yourself a Christian. It also means you don’t have to despair when the things you trusted turn out to be fragile. Your friendships, your job, even your sense of who you are were never meant to carry that much weight.

For now, we’re living in between. The booth is raised, but friendships still fade and jobs still feel shaky. The difference is that you’re not holding yourself up while you wait.

What still feels unfinished is whether you actually live like the restoration is already real.

***Where are you still acting like you have to hold it all up yourself?***
